Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:12.009 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  how do you teach someone to play your  game in an effective way how do you make  it engaging and comprehensible and is it  possible to organically weave a  controlled environment into the story  and setting without making it feel  forced or disconnected from the rest of  the game these are all questions devs  have been dealing with since the dawn of  gaming and the solutions differ from  genre to genre and changed as technology  progressed for the first Zelda game ever  made the solution was well you write a  freaking novel take all the information  a player needs to know including the  Overworld map controls enemy and item  catalogs and even the bulk of the story  and slap it into a booklet provided with  the cartridge it's about as old school  as it gets and not many games still do  it this way but honestly I think it  still has its charm as for the game  itself the player was given exactly one  screen to get to grips with the controls  before being confronted by enemies two  if you count the cave where link obtains  the first sword but there was nothing  restricting you from venturing into  Hyrule proper without it and the sword  cordless run has in fact been a  self-imposed challenge for seasoned  Zelda Veterans for a very long time as  if the game wasn't hard enough already  in A Link to the Past the freedom to  explore right from the get-go was  heavily toned down and players were  instead confined to Hyrule Castle which  served as the game's tutorial section  you had to fight and puzzle your way  through before being set free while the  castle does have a few branching paths  and optional areas it was still a  controlled and predominantly linear  experience quite the contrast from the  first title following A Link to the Past  a new standard for the game's starting  area would be set and repeated for most  Mainline Zelda titles in the years to  come instead of being airdropped into  the world and left your own devices or  navigating a linear Dungeness  environment the hero would often take  his first steps inside of a safe Zone  the circumstances do vary slightly from  game to game but the pattern is almost  identical you start out in a town often  times Link's Hometown you can't leave  until you've completed or triggered  certain story beats there's no enemies  to seek out and fight until the game  dictates as such you talk to NPCs who  explain some basic control or gameplay  elements give story Exposition or  provide menial tasks for link to do  until finally when you progress the  story enough a larger conflict or Quest  will be presented which will then send  link on his way and the true Adventure  Starts Now this was primarily a 3D Zelda  thing but there's no shortage of 2D or  top-down installments who adapted this  formula as well in fact Ocarina of Time  technically didn't even start this trend  but rather Link's Awakening did granted  you can get out of town and be on your  way in under a few minutes if you know  where to go so it wasn't nearly as  restrictive but still this linear and  formulaic approach to kicking off his  Zelda Adventure seemed to get taken to  the extreme with every new entry  Twilight Princess and Skyward Sword  especially are Infamous for their  long-winded confining story-driven  opening segments with lots of mandatory  tasks and dialogue to get through before  finally being sent on your way this  approach has garnered a lot of criticism  over the years particularly the argument  that opening segments like these can be  pretty jarring on repeat playthroughs  and to an extent I do agree with that  sentiment though my stance on it is a  bit more nuanced I don't actually hate  this way of starting out a Zelda game in  fact I can totally see why the  developers were coming from it can be an  effective way to do some World building  and create relationships and goals for  link and by extension the player to care  about and accomplish prior to kicking  off the main narrative the opening to  Twilight Princess and Skyward Sword may  be a bit of a drag to get through but  they also happen to be the games where I  care the most about the many Side  characters and by the end of the story  it's not just the heroes come a long way  but his friends as well from a father  who lost his children and joins a  resistance group in castletown an  insecure kid who finds the courage to  protect others a former bully who turns  into a hero himself to an insolent and  opportunistic sidekick who in time grows  to respect and care for the hero so no I  don't think this approach to opening up  a Zelda story was bad per se its  execution could have used some work and  an option to skip most of this fluff on  a second playthrough would have been  welcome but it did serve a purpose for  these particular stories that Twilight  Princess been the only game in the  series to start out this way I'm not too  sure if people would have complained  about it nearly as much the problem is  that almost every Mainline Zelda title  before and after was essentially  repeating the exact same Trope of a hero  from humble beginnings having his  peaceful life disrupted by some external  factor and then having a greater Destiny  thrown upon them again none of this is  bad on an individual level whether it's  saving your sister childhood friends or  love interests they're all effective  motivators to get link off his ass and  do what he does best burglary and  vandalism oh and saving the world of  course however when you do the same  thing enough time sooner or later it  does become formulaic and tiresome then  breath of the wild came along a game  Dead set on breaking conventions and in  some respects even taking a step back to  the roots of the series The developers  basically took a hatchet to many of the  series tropes and the quote-unquote  tutorial was no exception the great  Plateau is regarded as one of the best  opening segments to any Zelda game and  even games in general and for good  reason it ditches the metric tons of  force dialogue cutscenes and meaningless  chores in favor of a more open  free-flowing tutorial with a lot of  optional story and rewards and multiple  ways to overcome the same obstacle there  are still guidelines present here but  they aren't as obvious the so-called  Invisible Hand of Nintendo which is  mostly noticed only by those intent on  dissecting breath of the wild from a  game design perspective you know the  king case Mark Browns and Joseph  Andersons Among Us of course they  couldn't go all the way back to the NES  and just start the game with links  standing in the middle of a field  without any explanation or Exposition  whatsoever because as much as people may  think this was 100 done on purpose back  then the truth is that it was also just  part of the limitations of the medium  and because Zelda wasn't a massive story  driven lore-filled IP yet at this stage  I mean the entire story and goal can be  explained in just a few paragraphs so  there was essentially no need for  extensive dialogue and cutscenes however  with every entry the series did become  more story focused and lore heavy and  although breath the wild story is  fragmented and takes place more or less  in the background it still required some  kind of setup for Link's Quest and the  great Plateau serves that function  masterfully it teaches you most of what  you need to know about both the gameplay  and the main plot threads of what  happened to Hyrule during Link's absence  and the danger that still lurks deep  inside Hyrule castle and it does all of  this while wasting as little of your  time as possible making a second  playthrough a lot less cumbersome upon  exiting the shrine of resurrection and  stepping onto the great Plateau there's  only four mandatory dialogue exchanges  all of which are brief and a total of  three noteworthy cutscenes which are all  skippable even when viewing them for the  first time anything else is either  completely optional or shows up as a  prompt which barely interrupts the  gameplay which is in stark contrast to  previous titles Like the Wind Waker and  Twilight Princess where you were  essentially locked into obligatory  weapon tutorials the best aspect of the  great Plateau by far is just how vast  and non-restrictive it is sure you are  technically still in a controlled  environment barred from the rest of the  map but it's kinda hard to complain  about being confined to one area when  set area is roughly about the same size  as the entire world map of Ocarina of  Time as many have pointed out in the  past the cleverness of the plateau lies  in the fact that it's basically a  miniature version of larger Hyrule it  does still leave room for surprises but  it covers all the basics enemy camps  natural hazards the effects of extreme  temperature using the environment to  your advantage hunting Gathering and  cooking steep Cliffs teaching you about  stamina management it's all here the  plateau also mirrors the quest to free  the Divine beasts albeit on a smaller  scale after talking to impa you get the  option to seek out four points on the  map where the beasts are located in any  order you please on the plateau after  talking to the old man there are four  shrines which can be tackled in any  order too out of the four beasts farruta  is purposely positioned to be closest to  Link's current position and it's clear  that the Zelda team accounted for the  fact that most people will likely go  here first because the road to Zora's  domain features some of the weakest  enemy types likewise the closest of the  four shrines also happens to be the  easiest to get to with barely any  resistance while the road to the other  three are more treacherous and present  their own unique challenges still you  can completely ignore the intended path  which the devs accounted for as the old  man will always show up in whichever  Shrine you tackle first another more  subtle thing the plateau does is it  teaches you how Hyrule is meant to be  explored even if you don't realize it  for example moments before Zelda tells  link to head for the point marked on the  map the old man draws your attention to  the ruins of the Temple of Time he  doesn't say you have to go there he just  points it out to you like hey look at  these old ruins aren't they cool wink  wink so right away you have two points  of interest one with an objective marker  and one without it which only stands out  visually if you shoot straight for the  objective you'll encounter a bit more  hardship than you would have if you  checked out the temple first all the  enemies up to this point have been  singular one-on-one encounters wielding  sticks and clubs only but the road to  the sheikah tower will set you on a  collision course with the first enemy  groups as well as the first projectile  wielding and shield carrying enemies  which for an office player could be a  problem however if you visited and  properly explored the Temple of Time  first you would have been rewarded with  better armor a sword and one or two bows  and some arrows making the enemy  encounters on the path to the objective  significantly easier to deal with it  lets you know purely through World  design that it's okay and even  recommended not to blindly follow  objective markers and instead Venture  off the beaten path as you'll be  rewarded for your curiosity and that's  basically breath of the wild in a  nutshell right you aim for a specific  goal and on the way there you get  distracted by a million different things  and before you know it hours have passed  and you're not even close to that  initial goal you've set out to  accomplish so yeah suffice to say the  great Plateau is a master class in game  design and I will never get tired of  exploring it and analyzing all of its  intricacies it stands tall as my  favorite opening to any open world game  I've ever played so far and I've got  quite a few under my belt by this point  sure stepping out of Vault 101 while  your eyes try to adjust to the first  sunlight to ever hit your reticles was  amazing my first steps onto Lim grave in  Elden ring was also nothing short of  breathtaking but nothing comes close to  the pure ecstasy I felt during the first  hours exploring the great plateau in  breath of the wild it truly felt like  Nintendo finally got it it would have  been so easy for them to fall back into  old habits and bar off major sections of  the plateau in typical Zelda Flash like  I don't know you cannot access Mount  hylia because the cliffs are too high  and there's a boulder Block in the  entrance forcing you to get the remote  bombs first before you can access the  other two shrines but they didn't they  stayed committed to Breaking these types  of conventions and break them they sure  did so after all that gushing over the  great Plateau what about the upcoming  sequel how will it handle its tutorial  section leave it to a monster maze video  to get to the main topic 10 minutes in  right it feels kind of weird that I'm  actually excited about something like  the opening segment to a Zelda game  which prior to breath of the wild was  oftentimes the part I was just trying to  be done with so I could finally go on  the real Adventure it goes without  saying that the plateau still exists in  a new game since well it's the same  Hyrule but I think I'm not alone in  saying that I highly doubt we will start  the adventure here once again back in  the old days of the channel you know  when I still talk like an angry Dutch  guy this monster he just created will  serve as the first tutorial enemy for  link to test out his new green hand  ability I proposed that the cave system  Link and Zelda are seen traversing in  the first teaser could serve service the  tutorial section maybe after Zelda falls  into the abyss and Ganondorf has taken  off we'll have to escape the underground  and make our way back to the surface  somehow which would obviously be a more  linear approach to a tutorial akin to  the Vermin infested sewers over Link to  the Past now of course I made this  proposal long before we knew that the  sequel would feature a big emphasis on  Sky traversal and a lot more Revelations  have come out since then now it seems  increasingly unlikely that we'll take  our first steps deep underground  somewhere unless maybe it's for story  setup somewhere along the lines of the  opening to The Last of Us where you  don't actually play the game to its core  mechanics yet but are more so just  steering the character from cutscene to  cutscene for the sake of immersion but  that's not really a Zelda thing to do if  that makes any sense it also wouldn't be  much of a tutorial because we already  know from the footage and the patterns  issued by Nintendo that a big chunk of  Tears of the Kingdom's gameplay will be  centered around skydiving aerial combat  and Link's Afflicted arm and the new  abilities it brings with it the first  two of which are kinda hard to do on the  ground and as for the magical bracelet  it's apparent that he won't be in  possession of it and until after  Ganondorf attacks the hero which looks  to be taking place in a cutscene we also  know that this attack knocks him  unconscious as he's seen laying in the  dark on some kind of altar with the new  bracelet on his arm the symbols on this  altar also correspond to the ones we've  seen on some of the architecture in the  Sky Realm which were never seen on the  surface of Hyrule so maybe instead of  deep underground link comes too inside  of a building located on one of these  islands maybe this building I don't know  and the scene of Link pushing these  massive doors open will be this game's  equivalent of the moment link first ran  onto the cliff overlooking the great  Plateau I'm not saying it's impossible  that we may control link for the first  time in this underground setting maybe  we'll have some sort of pre-ordained  boss fight against Ganondorf one which  link is destined to lose which is a  classic way of instilling a sense of  fear and helplessness into the player  through gameplay instead of a cutscene  but again I wouldn't call this a  tutorial or opening area and more so an  interactive story setup if I had to  guess I'd say right now the most logical  option for a controlled environment  where the player will get the grips with  the game's core mechanics would be one  of the the islands in the sky one which  similar to The Great Plateau will have  ample amounts of explorable space but  link won't be able to leave the island  until he completes a number of Story  related tasks and has collected some  sort of key item just like the  paraglider in a previous title speaking  of the paraglider the one we see him use  in some of the teaser footage while  similar is not the same one from the  previous adventure and since link seems  to have been stripped of most of his  equipment including his clothes they  might just do a repeat here after all  without a paraglider jumping back down  to the surface would probably be  ill-advised so maybe escaping this  starting island is as simple as finding  the paraglider again which granted  wouldn't be very original but I mean if  it works it works right in the latest  teaser we can actually see a large  island or island group that looks big  enough to serve as an opening area it's  difficult to determine its exact size  given the perspective and lack of  reference but it looks like it could be  about the same size as the great Plateau  if not bigger the idea of Link being  stranded on a large island in the sky  after being injured by malice and then  having to look for a way down seems like  a pretty fitting introduction to me  after all the sky is the big new  addition to this iteration of Hyrule and  since we've spent so much time on the  surface in breath of the wild already it  just wouldn't make a lot of sense to me  to start the game down here again I know  not everyone who picks up tears of the  Kingdom will have played the original  but breath of the wild was a worldwide  phenomena and one of the best-selling  games of all time so I'd wager that the  majority of tiers of the king themselves  will be returning players who are  familiar with the original so with all  that said here's a quick summary of my  prediction for tears of the Kingdom's  opening we see the events from the first  teaser play out on the ground Link and  Zelda find ganon's corpse Zelda gets  yielded into the abyss while link  powerless to stop it gets his arm and  sword charred by malice knocking him  unconscious after this The Entity of  this bracelet originally belonged to  somehow takes link up into the sky to  heal his wounds and subsequently passes  the bracelet onto him to put a damper on  the infection spreading across his arm  which also makes him inherit some of the  powers and abilities this entity once  possessed when link comes to he will be  indoors and doesn't realize where he is  until he opens the front door to the  structure he's in as he steps out into  the daylight the Sky Realm is first  revealed and Link realizing he's stuck  up here goes on to explore this confined  area to look for something that can take  him down safely either in the form of  one or more powers for his new bracelet  or by obtaining something else like a  paraglider and pick up a few starting  weapons and gear on the way as well as  for why he would want to go back to the  surface as soon as possible we of course  don't know for sure but I can imagine  that he'd want to inform his friends on  the surface of what happened down in the  cave with Ganondorf and such after all  Link and Zelda were alone on this Quest  as far as we know and Zelda disappeared  and Link was knocked out so it's likely  that at this stage nobody except link  and Ganondorf know what happened to  Zelda while stuck up here link will also  fight his first grounded enemies either  on the island itself or inside one of  the many buildings scenes scattered  about which may act as many dungeons of  sorts that house the things he needs to  get back down to Hyrule like with the  great Plateau once he finds what he  needs link will finally be able to jump  down safely and leave the tutorial area  the fallback down to the surface is a  long and extensive one which may take a  couple of minutes and would be a perfect  opportunity to instruct the player on  the new skydiving mechanics like  controlling your direction and changing  poses they may even want to introduce  the first Airborne enemies on the way  down and teach you about aerial combat  like how to use the bow in free fall  which we know from the patterns will be  a new gameplay Edition as well finally  the hero will reach the surface back in  familiar territory and from here well  we'll just have to wait and see grocery  shopping perhaps but that's how I  currently think the first few hours or  so will more or less play out I could be  completely wrong about all of this but  hey that's why it's called a prediction  as for my final wishes regarding tears  of the Kingdom's opening and the game as  a whole for that matter I hope the game  will have a strong beginning but also  leave some room for growth amazement and  progression despite all the praise I've  given to the great plateau in this video  it did have one major flaw and that flaw  as ridiculous as it may sound is that it  was just too good so good in fact that  the first few hours of the game are the  ones I personally have the Fondest  Memories of now don't get me wrong I'm  not saying the rest of the game is bad  far from it but for some reason it did  feel like breath of the Wild's  experience kinda plateaued after the  great Plateau there's even some great  mechanics utilized on the plateau that  became pretty much redundant afterwards  in large part due to the paraglider the  game did have its fair share of high  points after the first few hours like  entering your first Divine Beast even  tight Island seeing a dragon for the  first time and storming the imposing  Hyrule Castle but I don't know it still  felt to me like with the great Plateau  the game may have peaked a little too  early for its own good I realize all too  well that this is a very subjective take  in your experience and opinions May  differ greatly but this is how I feel  about it when I think back to my first  playthrough of the game it's one aspect  I think Elden ring greatly improved upon  like I said before stepping into Lim  grave and seeing the gorgeous landscape  was awesome but I actually wasn't all  that Blown Away by Eldon ring in the  first few hours to be completely honest  I didn't hate it but I also didn't feel  the same sense of Amazement as I did did  back in 2017 however one major  difference is that Elden ring only  seemed to get better and better crazier  and crazier the moment you think you've  seen it all that you've reached the  absolute peak of the experience the game  throws something even more mind-blowing  your way it was kind of nuts and I hope  tears of the Kingdom can achieve  something similar a solid opening that  gets you excited but is also restrained  enough to leave some room for much much  greater things to come and that  concludes today's video thank you so  much as always for watching I feel like  I can never say it enough you guys  showing up time and time again really  makes my day the road to tears of the  Kingdom has been a long one but our  patience will finally pay off very soon  a special thanks to my mods Discord  community patreons and channel members  and I hope to see you in the next video  this is Dawn signing off and have a good  one  [Music]  foreign  [Music]

